"""Original editorial content. HTML fragments are trusted, repository-owned source."""
ARTICLES = []
def article(slug, title, category, summary, sections, diagram, question, code=None):
    ARTICLES.append(dict(slug=slug, title=title, category=category, summary=summary, sections=sections, diagram=diagram, question=question, code=code))

article('kafka-business-problems', 'Understanding Kafka Through Real Business Problems', 'Apache Kafka',
'Choose event streaming by the decision deadline, replay requirement, and cost of coupling—not by message volume alone.', [
('Begin with the time between an event and a decision', '''A telecom usage event is useful for billing tomorrow and for fraud detection now. A nightly export can satisfy the first requirement while making the second impossible. Before choosing Kafka, write down who needs the event, the latest useful arrival time, and what happens if a consumer is unavailable. Those three answers define the service you need more precisely than “real time.”

In banking, an authorization request belongs on a bounded request-response path with an explicit timeout and a system of record. Publishing the resulting authorization event lets risk analytics, notifications, and reconciliation evolve independently. Kafka can distribute that fact; it should not silently become the authority for the account balance. The application still needs rules for concurrent updates, deduplication, and reconciliation.'''),
('Scheduled batch versus an event log', '''A scheduled batch pipeline asks what changed since its last watermark. It is economical when decisions tolerate delay and the source supports consistent extraction. It needs overlap handling, late-arrival rules, and a way to replay a failed interval. Streaming replaces the schedule with continuously advancing positions in an event log. It lowers waiting time, but introduces always-running consumers, lag monitoring, schema compatibility, and retention planning.

Consider an analytics team that needs yesterday’s revenue before 08:00. A small, reliable batch job may be a better investment than a streaming platform. Now add customer notifications within a minute and three independently deployed consumers. An append-only log becomes more attractive because each consumer can advance at its own rate. The comparison is operating cost against an actual latency requirement, not old technology against new technology.'''),
('What Kafka supplies—and what remains your job', '''Kafka provides a replicated partitioned log, consumer positions, retention, and independent consumption. The producer can continue while a reporting consumer is offline, provided the outage does not exhaust storage or outlast retention. A replay can rebuild a view from retained records without asking the source database to reproduce every historical change.

Kafka does not validate that an amount is correct, grant business-level idempotency, guarantee global ordering across partitions, or fix an unavailable downstream API. A malformed event can be replayed perfectly and remain wrong. Add event identifiers, schema evolution rules, ownership, and source-to-sink reconciliation. Treat those as part of the architecture, not paperwork after launch.'''),
('A practical adoption decision', '''For the telecom example, define a freshness SLO such as a stated percentage of eligible events becoming available to the fraud feature pipeline within a chosen deadline. Separate event time from ingestion time so delayed source delivery is visible. Track oldest unprocessed event age, duplicate outcomes, recovery time, and cost per million useful events.

Pilot one event type with a deliberate consumer outage and a replay. Measure catch-up capacity while new traffic continues. If arrival rate is 1,000 records/s and sustainable consumption is 1,200 records/s, only 200 records/s are available to clear backlog. A 720,000-record backlog therefore needs about an hour, assuming stable rates. That recovery budget may change the capacity decision more than steady-state latency.''')],
['Source transaction', 'Durable event log', 'Independent risk / analytics / notification consumers'],
'Which decision in your organization becomes less valuable while a batch schedule waits?')

article('kafka-architecture', 'Kafka Architecture Explained: Partitions, Replicas and KRaft', 'Apache Kafka',
'Follow a record from producer to partition leader to consumer, then separate data replication from metadata consensus.', [
('The data path: topics, partitions, brokers', '''A topic names a stream; partitions divide its log into independent ordered sequences. Brokers store replicas of those partitions. The producer learns metadata, chooses a partition, and sends to its leader. Followers replicate the leader’s log. A replication factor of three means three replicas total, not three followers. Spread replicas across failure domains when the deployment supports rack awareness.

An offset is a position inside one topic partition. Offset 50 in partition 0 has no ordering relationship with offset 50 in partition 1. A stable non-null key normally routes related records to the same partition under the selected partitioner. It does not imply a key range, and changing the number of partitions can change the key-to-partition mapping. Plan that change if per-entity order matters.'''),
('Replication and acknowledgement are a joint policy', '''The in-sync replica set tracks replicas keeping up within configured limits. With acks=all, the leader waits for the required in-sync replication acknowledgement; min.insync.replicas determines when writes must be rejected because too few replicas remain. With RF=3 and minimum ISR=2, the cluster can usually continue after one replica loss, but placement and concurrent failures matter.

Acks=all with RF=1 still means one copy. It is useful in a local lab but gives no broker failure tolerance. Increasing replication consumes storage and network capacity. Avoid treating replica count alone as a durability proof: examine acknowledgement settings, unclean leader election policy, disk behavior, and disaster-recovery boundaries together.'''),
('Consumers, groups, and committed positions', '''Within a conventional consumer group, a partition is assigned to one active consumer at a time. Consumers in different groups can independently read the same records. Adding consumers beyond the partition count does not create more partition-level parallelism. A rebalance changes assignments; it is not evidence that records have disappeared.

The consumer’s current position and its committed offset are different. A commit records the next offset to read after recovery. If processing succeeds but the commit fails, work may repeat. If the commit succeeds before processing, work may be skipped after a crash. This is why offset management belongs next to sink-side correctness in the design.'''),
('Retention and compaction are different promises', '''Time- or size-based deletion bounds the retained log. A consumer offline longer than that window may lose the ability to recover from its committed offset. Compaction eventually removes superseded values for the same key; it does not immediately turn the log into one row per key. Tombstones represent deletions and have their own retention concerns.

Use deletion retention for a bounded event history and compaction for reconstructing keyed state when that model fits. Neither replaces a backup policy. Budget disk for replicas, segments awaiting cleanup, indexes, and operational headroom rather than assuming that a configured retention duration is an exact disk cap.'''),
('KRaft is the metadata plane', '''Modern Kafka uses a KRaft controller quorum to manage cluster metadata through a replicated metadata log. The active controller coordinates metadata changes; controller followers provide quorum continuity. Partition leaders handle data traffic and are conceptually separate from the active metadata controller. A healthy controller quorum does not prove that a particular data partition is healthy.

Kafka 4.x no longer uses ZooKeeper. Older deployments may still have ZooKeeper-based operational procedures, so do not copy their failure diagnosis into a KRaft runbook. A one-node combined broker/controller is appropriate for the lab. Production role separation and quorum membership depend on the Kafka version and availability requirements. During an incident, identify which plane is failing before restarting anything.''')],
['Producer → partition leader → follower replicas', 'Controller quorum → cluster metadata', 'Consumer group → assigned partitions → committed offsets'],
'If the controller quorum is healthy but one partition cannot elect a leader, which evidence would you inspect next?')

article('kafka-java-producer-consumer', 'Kafka Producer and Consumer Tutorial in Java', 'Apache Kafka',
'Run a bounded Java lab that creates a topic, sends keyed orders, checks partition routing, and verifies committed offsets.', [
('Prerequisites and scope', '''Use Kafka 4.1.1 in KRaft mode, its matching Java client, JDK 17 or 21, and Maven 3.9.x. The accompanying source is intentionally small enough to inspect. It creates a unique topic with two partitions and replication factor one, sends six synthetic orders, consumes them with a unique group, checks routing and commits, then deletes the topic. Run it only against a disposable local broker with topic-create and topic-delete permissions.

This is an executable correctness exercise, not a throughput benchmark or a banking consumer. There is no external database and the business side effect is a console message. The explicit boundary matters: successful offset assertions cannot prove an atomic write to a system that the example never calls.'''),
('Start a local KRaft broker and build', '''Download the Kafka 4.1.1 binary distribution from Apache and verify its published SHA-512 checksum. From its directory, use the commands below once for a fresh data directory, then keep the broker running in that terminal. Do not reformat an existing broker’s storage. The combined-role server in the distribution is for development.

In a second terminal, build examples/kafka from this portfolio repository. Maven resolves the pinned client and plugin versions. The program’s bootstrap address defaults to localhost:9092; pass another address as its first argument only if the broker advertises addresses reachable from your client. A successful bootstrap connection is insufficient when advertised broker addresses are wrong.'''),
('Read the producer policy', '''String serializers make keys and payloads visible. The customer key keeps a customer’s orders in the same partition while the partition count and partitioner remain stable. Two keys can hash to the same partition; the test does not assume that two keys occupy two different partitions. Explicit acks=all and enable.idempotence=true demonstrate the producer policy even where defaults already enable it.

Waiting on each send future makes this lab easy to reason about and exposes broker errors to the caller. It deliberately sacrifices throughput. The lab sets linger.ms=5 and request.timeout.ms=10000 explicitly; delivery.timeout.ms=30000 exceeds their sum and bounds producer delivery attempts. the future wait is slightly longer so a delivery failure can surface. Idempotence protects against certain producer retries inside the producer session. It does not deduplicate a business event submitted again by a new application run.'''),
('Read the consumer policy', '''The consumer subscribes as a group member and lets Kafka assign partitions. auto.offset.reset=earliest applies only when the group has no usable committed position; it does not rewind an existing group on every restart. Automatic commits are disabled. After processing a poll batch, the lab commits the highest processed offset plus one for each partition.

Processing and committing are intentionally ordered. In production, replace the console side effect with an idempotent durable write before committing. If a write succeeds and the process dies before the commit, replay is expected. Store a stable event ID or a partition-offset identity with the durable outcome. Do not mark a failed record as complete merely to keep lag low.'''),
('Expected results and failure diagnosis', '''Topic names and offsets vary by run. Expect six Produced lines, six Processed lines, and the final PASS message. The lab asserts six distinct payloads, stable key routing, and committed next offsets; a missing record or mismatch exits nonzero. Compare the output with the checked-in validation record rather than assuming a successful compilation means messages flowed.

Connection timeouts suggest a stopped broker, an unreachable advertised listener, or a wrong port. Topic authorization failures require permissions, not more retries. A consumer that sees no data may be in an old group with committed positions, reading another topic, or waiting for assignment. This lab uses fresh identifiers to remove those ambiguities. If a run fails before cleanup, inspect and remove only its uniquely named orders-lab topic.''')],
['Admin creates 2-partition topic', 'Producer → keyed orders → broker', 'Consumer processes → commits next offsets → assertions'],
'Where would you put a deduplication record if the sink were a relational database?', 'kafka')

article('kafka-reliability', 'Kafka Reliability and Message Delivery: Follow the Failure Window', 'Apache Kafka',
'Use crash timing to reason about at-most-once, at-least-once, idempotence, transactions, and dead-letter handling.', [
('The commit boundary defines replay', '''Imagine a consumer that writes an invoice and then commits its offset. Crash after the write but before the commit and the invoice event returns. Commit first, then crash before the write, and the invoice is lost from the application’s perspective. These two windows explain more than a delivery-label checklist.

At-most-once processing accepts loss to avoid replay, often by advancing the position before the side effect. At-least-once processing accepts replay to avoid that loss window. Neither label is useful without the boundary: a broker acknowledgement, a consumer fetch, a durable sink write, and a visible business outcome are separate events.'''),
('Idempotence is not a business uniqueness rule', '''An idempotent producer lets the broker recognize protocol-level retries using producer identity and sequence information. It prevents certain duplicate log appends caused by retrying a send. It does not know whether two separately constructed “charge customer” events describe the same business request.

For an external database sink, put an event ID uniqueness constraint and the state change inside the same database transaction. A retry can then return the existing outcome. Storing a deduplication marker before a non-atomic API call creates a new loss window. Expiring the marker too early also allows old replays to become new side effects. Align its retention with your replay contract.'''),
('Exactly-once has a bounded scope', '''Kafka transactions can atomically publish output records and consumed offsets in a read-process-write pipeline when configured correctly. Consumers of transactional output need isolation.level=read_committed to avoid exposing aborted records. A stable transactional.id supports producer fencing, but concurrent instances must not accidentally share ownership of the same transactional identity.

This guarantee does not automatically extend to an email service or an arbitrary database. Kafka Streams can provide exactly-once processing within its supported Kafka/state-store model; an external payment API remains outside that atomic boundary. Use an outbox, an idempotent sink, or a reconciliation workflow, and state the residual failure mode plainly.'''),
('Retries, poison records, and dead-letter topics', '''Retry transient failures with a bounded policy and observable backoff. Repeatedly retrying a deterministic schema error blocks the partition without making the record valid. A dead-letter topic can preserve the original event, error class, original topic-partition-offset, event timestamp, and attempt count for later repair.

Publishing to a dead-letter topic and committing the source offset must be coordinated. Two unrelated writes create either loss or duplicate quarantine records during a crash. A Kafka transaction can coordinate those Kafka operations; external audit stores need their own consistency design. Quarantine changes processing order and business completeness, so expose it in the dataset’s quality status rather than counting the event as successfully delivered.'''),
('A failure test worth running', '''Use a disposable environment and a sink with a unique event ID. Kill the consumer after the sink commit but before the offset commit, then restart it. The expected result is a repeated delivery with one durable business effect. Repeat with broker unavailability during offset commit and with a malformed payload. Record both processing attempts and outcomes.

The Java lab verifies clean-path offsets and routing. It does not execute these transaction, crash, or dead-letter scenarios; they remain explicitly unexecuted exercises. Do not promote its PASS line into a claim of exactly-once production behavior. The business measure is duplicate or missing outcomes after recovery, not simply a flat lag chart.''')],
['Read event → durable sink write', 'Crash window → replay possible', 'Commit next offset → recover from saved position'],
'What is the atomic boundary of your system’s exactly-once claim?')

article('kafka-troubleshooting', 'Kafka Production Troubleshooting: Diagnose Before Restarting', 'Apache Kafka',
'Connect lag, replica health, disk, network, and controller evidence to a safe next action.', [
('Start with impact and a time window', '''A lag alert is a symptom, not a diagnosis. Identify the group, topic, partitions, event-time freshness, and business deadline. Record when the problem began and whether producer traffic changed. Compare rates over the same interval: a rising end offset with a flat committed offset differs from a quiet producer and a stalled source system.

Preserve evidence before a restart. Capture group state, assignment changes, broker logs, ISR membership, disk capacity, and recent deployments. A restart may erase a transient signal or trigger more rebalances. Prioritize whether data is unavailable, at risk, or merely arriving later than its deadline.'''),
('Lag and slow consumers', '''Use kafka-consumer-groups.sh --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 --describe --group GROUP. CURRENT-OFFSET is committed progress, LOG-END-OFFSET is the partition end, and LAG is their difference. It is a count, not seconds of business delay. A consumer committing in large batches can look stalled even while processing, so compare application throughput and last successful sink writes.

If lag is concentrated on one partition, inspect key skew and per-record processing cost before adding instances. If all partitions slow together, inspect a shared sink, network path, or deployment. Scale consumers only while unassigned partition capacity remains. Increasing max.poll.interval.ms may prevent a rebalance during long work, but can also delay recognizing a stuck member; reduce batch size or redesign processing before treating it as a cure.'''),
('Broker failure and under-replicated partitions', '''Describe the topic and inspect leaders, replica lists, and ISR. A healthy three-replica partition normally lists three ISR members; an ISR shorter than the replica list signals lost replication redundancy. A missing leader is a more urgent availability problem. Correlate the affected broker IDs with process health, disk I/O latency, network errors, and host events.

Do not force an unclean leader election just to turn an alert green. It can trade unavailable data for lost acknowledged history. Restore the failed replica or connectivity where possible, estimate catch-up pressure, and follow the deployment’s documented recovery policy. Check whether min.insync.replicas is intentionally rejecting writes to protect durability.'''),
('Disk pressure, network faults, and rebalance storms', '''For disk pressure, collect df -h, per-log-directory usage, segment growth, retention settings, and cleanup errors. Avoid deleting log segment files manually. Expanding storage can buy time; lowering retention may permanently remove replay history. Include consumer outage budgets before making that trade-off.

For network faults, compare client timeout logs with broker request latency and connection churn. A reachable bootstrap port does not prove reachable advertised listeners. For rebalances, correlate member join/leave events with deployments, long processing intervals, GC pauses, and session failures. Staggering restarts may help availability, but does not correct a consumer that repeatedly exceeds its processing budget.'''),
('Controller and metadata issues', '''Use kafka-metadata-quorum.sh --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 describe --status on a compatible KRaft deployment. Inspect the leader, voters, high watermark, and follower lag with the version’s supported describe options. A missing quorum can block metadata changes even while some existing data paths continue working.

Distinguish metadata quorum lag from topic replication lag. Check controller logs and connectivity between quorum members before changing membership. Do not format storage or invent a new cluster ID during recovery. Version-specific membership procedures matter, especially when moving between static and dynamic quorums.'''),
('Verify recovery against the original symptom', '''The commands below were checked against the disposable Kafka 4.1.1 broker; no production outage was induced. The single-node lab cannot demonstrate replica-failure recovery. On a production incident, confirm stable assignments, restored redundancy, sustainable consumption above arrival rate, and event freshness returning within the agreed deadline.

Close with the cause, evidence, bounded change, and rollback conditions. “Restart fixed it” leaves the next responder without a model. Track recurrence and time to restore useful business data, not only time to restart the process.''')],
['Freshness / lag alert', 'Partition + client + broker evidence', 'Bounded change → rate recovery → business verification'],
'Which metric distinguishes a slow sink from a producer traffic surge in your runbook?', 'kafka-ops')

article('kafka-performance', 'Kafka Performance Optimization Is a Capacity Trade-off', 'Apache Kafka',
'Balance throughput, tail latency, recovery headroom, storage, and cost instead of copying a universal configuration.', [
('Set the workload and latency budget', '''Begin with record size distribution, records per second, key distribution, consumer count, and p95/p99 end-to-end latency. A benchmark made of tiny uniformly distributed messages will not predict a production stream dominated by a few large keys. Include serialization, sink work, and acknowledgement policy in the measured path.

Hold reliability settings constant while comparing performance. Turning off replication acknowledgements may improve a number while changing the failure contract. Report the sustained rate, tail latency, errors, and resources together. A saturated cluster that passes average throughput can still miss recovery deadlines.'''),
('Partition count and consumer parallelism', '''Partitions increase available parallelism, but also increase metadata, replica work, open segments, recovery tasks, and rebalance complexity. A hot business key remains on one partition regardless of how many consumers you add. Changing partition count can change keyed routing and requires an ordering migration plan.

Estimate per-partition processing capacity from the real sink, then leave headroom for a failed consumer or broker. If six consumers barely meet steady-state traffic, losing one may cause backlog growth. The correct count depends on both arrival rate and catch-up time after the expected outage, not just the number of CPU cores.'''),
('Batching, compression, and latency', '''batch.size bounds the target producer batch per partition, while linger.ms allows a bounded wait to collect more records. Larger batches can amortize requests and improve compression, but add waiting time and buffer pressure. Kafka defaults vary by version; specify the tested version and measure rather than repeating an old “zero linger” assumption.

Compression saves network and disk at a CPU cost. LZ4 and Zstandard are useful candidates, but payload entropy and batch sizes affect the result. Compare broker CPU, producer queue time, request latency, compression ratio, and consumer decompression cost. A compressor that wins on bytes may lose on an already CPU-limited workload.'''),
('Storage and replication arithmetic', '''For an illustrative uncompressed ingress of 10 MB/s and three days of retention, one copy requires about 2.592 TB using decimal units. RF=3 raises the logical replicated total to about 7.776 TB before compression, indexes, segment overhead, cleanup delay, and headroom. This is a planning estimate, not a promise about allocated disk.

Network replication and cross-zone placement can add cost beyond storage. Retention reduction saves capacity by removing history, so tie it to the longest supported outage and rebuild window. Also budget spare capacity for a replica catching up while live traffic continues. Running disks almost full turns routine recovery into an availability event.'''),
('An experiment with a decision rule', '''Replay the same representative dataset with a small matrix of batch sizes, linger values, compression choices, and consumer concurrency. Warm up the clients, run long enough to observe steady-state behavior, and repeat to expose variance. Reject candidates that violate tail-latency or durability requirements even when their peak throughput is higher.

Then test a controlled consumer outage and measure backlog clearance. Save configuration, software versions, hardware, rates, latency percentiles, errors, and cost assumptions with each result. These benchmark and failure experiments are not executed by the functional Java lab. Treat them as a workload-specific exercise; no universal performance uplift is claimed.''')],
['Record rate × size × retention', 'Replication + compression + headroom', 'Throughput / p99 latency / recovery / cost decision'],
'Would your cheapest steady-state configuration clear a one-hour backlog before the next business deadline?')

article('why-spark-exists', 'Why Spark Exists: Distributed Processing With a Deadline', 'Apache Spark & PySpark',
'Decide when distributed computation earns its coordination cost, using billing, reconciliation, and feature engineering examples.', [
('The problem is more than file size', '''A telecom billing reconciliation may need to join usage events, tariff versions, customer contracts, and adjustments before a fixed closing window. A banking risk team may rebuild features over months of transactions. In both cases, the constraint is not merely that data is “big.” It is that scan, join, aggregation, and recovery must fit a business deadline on an affordable platform.

A single machine can be the best solution while its memory, I/O, and recovery profile meet that deadline. Distributed processing earns its cost when work can be partitioned and coordinated faster or more reliably than one machine can handle. It adds network transfer, scheduling, serialization, and more ways for a component to fail. Start by measuring the current bottleneck rather than assuming a cluster is an upgrade.'''),
('What Spark changed', '''Spark represents transformations as a dependency graph, schedules parallel work over partitions, and can reuse intermediate data. Its SQL engine optimizes structured queries before execution. These capabilities make iterative processing and multi-step pipelines easier to express than repeatedly materializing every intermediate boundary through separate jobs.

Spark is not an in-memory-only system. It reads durable storage, writes outputs, spills during execution, and recomputes lost partitions when possible. Memory helps when working sets and reuse justify it; a job dominated by a large shuffle or remote storage can remain slow. “Add memory” is not a substitute for inspecting the plan and the data distribution.'''),
('A useful partition of responsibility', '''Keep durable input and output in a storage system with an explicit consistency and commit contract. Let Spark compute transformations, and let orchestration define schedules, dependencies, and recovery. Publish schema, freshness, and reconciliation checks as part of the dataset contract. A completed action says computation finished; it does not prove the result is fit for a decision.

For a daily revenue job, compare source totals, accepted records, quarantined records, and published totals for the same cutoff. Preserve the version of reference data used. If rerunning a day changes a historical number, the business needs to know whether that is a correction or an unintended non-deterministic join.'''),
('Use the local lab as a model, not a scale claim', '''The accompanying PySpark lab writes partitioned Parquet, selects one day and a subset of columns, joins a small dimension, and aggregates by team. It uses integer amounts so the expected totals are exact. The two resulting totals are Finance=200 and Networks=120. Those assertions check behavior, while the formatted physical plan reveals how Spark intends to do the work.

The fixture is deliberately tiny and local. It cannot establish cluster throughput, fault tolerance, or a return on infrastructure spend. Scale experiments should preserve representative key skew, row width, file layout, and sink behavior. Measure deadline compliance and cost per successful publication alongside runtime.''')],
['Business deadline + durable source data', 'Partitioned scan → parallel transformation → shuffle', 'Validated result → published decision input'],
'What evidence would make you choose a larger single machine instead of a Spark cluster?', 'spark')

article('spark-architecture', 'Spark Architecture: From a Lazy Plan to Jobs, Stages and Tasks', 'Apache Spark & PySpark',
'Locate a slow operation in the driver, scheduler, executors, or storage path before changing resources.', [
('Driver, executors, and the cluster manager', '''The driver hosts the application’s control flow, Spark session, query planning, and scheduling coordination. Executors run tasks, hold cached blocks, and use memory and local disk for execution. A cluster manager such as YARN or Kubernetes allocates the resources; it does not decide the semantics of your join.

Client versus cluster deploy mode determines where the driver runs. A notebook driver on a laptop and a driver running inside the cluster have different network and failure boundaries. If executors cannot reach the driver’s advertised address, adding executor memory will not fix registration failures. Diagnose the control path separately from data access.'''),
('Transformations defer work; actions request results', '''select, filter, and join describe a logical plan. An action such as collect, count, or write asks Spark to execute enough of that plan to produce an outcome. Lazy evaluation gives the optimizer room to prune columns, push filters, and reorganize operations. It does not mean nothing can happen before an action: schema inference and file discovery can still do work.

Avoid the absolute rule “one action equals one job.” SQL execution, broadcast preparation, subqueries, and adaptive execution can cause a user action to involve multiple jobs. In the UI, begin with the SQL execution and follow its associated jobs rather than guessing a count from the number of actions in the source code.'''),
('Jobs, stages, and tasks', '''A job schedules work needed by an action. Stages group work separated by shuffle dependencies; narrow transformations can often be pipelined within a stage. A task operates on one partition for that stage attempt. Its duration depends on the partition’s data and processing cost, not just its row count.

A wide dependency redistributes records across partitions, creating a synchronization and materialization boundary. If one map task is slow, downstream work may wait even while many executors are idle. Task retries and speculative attempts also mean that the number of task attempts can exceed the number of partitions. Compare attempts and successful outputs before interpreting the UI.'''),
('Follow the local lab through the UI and plan', '''In the orders lab, reading Parquet and filtering a day form a scan path. The small dimension is broadcast. Grouping by team introduces a shuffle to bring matching keys together. The formatted plan exposes the scan, broadcast exchange, join, and aggregate. AQE may adjust the final plan after runtime statistics arrive.

For a real incident, capture the SQL plan, execution ID, stage IDs, task duration distribution, input bytes, shuffle read/write, spill, and executor loss messages. A driver-side planning delay, a slow storage scan, and a single skewed reduce task require different changes. Use the graph to narrow the question before experimenting with executor counts.''')],
['Driver builds logical / physical plan', 'Cluster manager allocates executors', 'Action → jobs → stages → tasks over partitions'],
'How would you distinguish driver planning delay from slow executor work using the Spark UI?', 'spark')

article('spark-internals', 'Spark Internals: Why Shuffles and Partition Shape Dominate', 'Distributed Systems',
'Reason about narrow dependencies, redistribution, serialization, locality, and scheduling through the bytes each task must move.', [
('Partitioning is a distribution of work', '''A partition is a unit of parallel work, not a guarantee of equal load. Ten equally sized files may contain very different row widths or key distributions. Input partitions depend on file splits and source behavior; shuffle partitions depend on exchanges and adaptive decisions. Do not confuse storage partition directories with execution partitions.

A useful first measurement is the distribution of task duration, input bytes, and output bytes. If the slowest task reads ten times the median data, resource tuning may be hiding a layout problem. If bytes are similar but one task is much slower, inspect CPU cost, remote access, retries, and executor health.'''),
('Narrow and wide dependencies', '''A filter can process each input partition independently: a narrow dependency. A groupBy on a key generally needs records for that key to meet across input partitions: a wide dependency and usually an exchange. Spark serializes shuffle output, writes blocks, and fetches them on the receiving side. Network, disk, and memory pressure all participate.

Not every join requires shuffling both inputs. Broadcast joins replicate a sufficiently small build side; compatible existing partitioning can also remove exchanges. Inspect the physical plan rather than counting join calls. Conversely, a seemingly harmless repartition can force a full exchange even when the data was already adequately distributed.'''),
('Serialization and locality', '''JVM structured execution uses Spark’s internal representations to reduce object overhead. Crossing into a Python UDF can add serialization and process-boundary cost; prefer built-in SQL functions where they express the same logic. That is a measurement hypothesis, not a ban on Python UDFs. Some specialized logic legitimately needs them.

Locality preferences try to place work near available data or cached blocks. In object-storage deployments, local-disk locality may matter less than network path, request concurrency, and connector behavior. Waiting longer for locality can hurt a short job when the expected local read advantage is small. Read scheduler delay and task locality metrics together.'''),
('Scheduling, skew, and the long tail', '''The scheduler cannot divide one ordinary reduce task across idle cores simply because it is slow. A hot key can create a large partition that dominates completion time. AQE can mitigate some skewed joins, but it does not automatically solve every skewed aggregation. A business key with huge state still needs an algorithmic strategy.

Use partial aggregation, selective key salting with correct recombination, or separate processing for exceptional keys when justified. Each adds correctness work. In a revenue aggregation, compare results against an unsalted reference fixture and check nulls, duplicate dimension keys, and precision. Faster incorrect totals are an operational regression.'''),
('A disciplined experiment', '''Run the lab and inspect Exchange in explain output. Then change spark.sql.shuffle.partitions on a larger representative fixture, keeping input and resources fixed. Compare task-size spread, scheduling overhead, spill, and total runtime. Too few partitions can create oversized tasks; too many can turn scheduling and tiny output files into the bottleneck.

The checked-in lab validates the baseline plan and totals, not this scale experiment. Keep the experiment’s results separate and repeat it with the platform’s exact Spark build. Vendor patches, AQE behavior, storage connectors, and workload shape can change the best choice.''')],
['Narrow scan / filter within partitions', 'Exchange serializes and redistributes keys', 'Reduce tasks → skewed tail → completion time'],
'Would your slowest stage improve by moving fewer bytes or by allocating more cores?', 'spark')

article('spark-performance', 'Spark Performance Optimization: Read the Physical Plan First', 'Apache Spark & PySpark',
'Validate pruning, pushdown, join strategy, AQE, caching, and file layout against a reproducible PySpark result.', [
('A runnable baseline', '''Use the linked orders_lab.py with PySpark 3.5.7, Python 3.10–3.12, and JDK 17. The validation record states the actual runtime tested. The script creates its own temporary Parquet fixture and removes it afterward, so it can be rerun without relying on private data or a metastore. Install the pinned dependency in a virtual environment and run the commands below.

The fixture includes two days and two sectors. Filtering to November 14 and amounts of at least 100 excludes both a small order and a later-day order. A small sector-to-team dimension is joined, then amounts are summed. Expected results are Finance=200 and Networks=120; the script asserts them rather than asking the reader to eyeball a table.'''),
('Three different ways to read less', '''Partition pruning removes irrelevant directory partitions using the day predicate. Column pruning reads the needed columns rather than every field. Predicate pushdown lets the source filter or skip work using supported predicates and metadata. They are related optimizations with different evidence.

In formatted explain output, inspect PartitionFilters, ReadSchema, and PushedFilters. A listed pushed filter does not prove every row group was skipped; actual savings depend on file statistics and data distribution. Function-wrapped columns, incompatible types, or connector limitations can prevent the intended optimization. Compare scanned bytes and output rows before claiming an improvement.'''),
('Broadcast joins and AQE', '''The explicit broadcast hint in the lab is safe because the dimension is tiny and controlled. In production, verify the serialized size, duplicate-key behavior, and per-executor memory budget before forcing a broadcast. A large replicated build side can turn a network optimization into executor OOM. Accurate statistics help Spark choose without an unconditional hint.

AQE can coalesce shuffle partitions and change eligible join strategies using runtime statistics. Inspect both initial and final plans after an action. AQE does not make all skew or poor source layouts disappear. Compare the longest tasks and spill metrics before and after a change, and retain a correctness assertion on the output.'''),
('Caching, memory, and file sizing', '''Cache a dataset when it is reused enough to repay materialization and memory pressure. A one-pass scan often gets no benefit. Materialize deliberately, observe storage usage, and unpersist after reuse. Cached blocks compete with execution memory; caching everything can increase eviction, spill, and GC.

Aim for file sizes that balance parallel reads against per-file listing and task overhead for your connector and workload. There is no universal target. Reducing output partitions can shrink the file count but create a long single-task tail. Too many partition directories also inflate metadata work. Measure listing time, scan throughput, task count, and downstream query behavior together.'''),
('Choose a change by its evidence', '''Capture the baseline input snapshot, Spark version, configuration, physical plan, runtime, task percentiles, shuffle bytes, spill, and result totals. Change one meaningful variable, rerun, and compare. For skew, inspect key-frequency distribution and consider preaggregation or a targeted strategy rather than blindly increasing every shuffle count.

The local lab verifies result correctness and the expected plan features. Its four-row fixture cannot establish a speedup. Benchmark claims require representative scale and repeated measurements, and production changes require the platform’s own compatibility and rollout checks. The operational goal is a reliable publication deadline at acceptable cost.''')],
['Prune partitions + columns + eligible predicates', 'Broadcast small dimension → aggregate exchange', 'Check final plan + output totals + runtime evidence'],
'Which plan feature would prove your filter reduced input work rather than only filtering after the scan?', 'spark')

article('spark-troubleshooting', 'Spark Troubleshooting: Turn Symptoms Into Testable Hypotheses', 'Apache Spark & PySpark',
'A six-part investigation method for memory failures, shuffle delays, skew, GC, failed stages, contention, and small files.', [
('Executor out-of-memory', '''Symptom: an executor disappears, tasks retry, or logs show heap exhaustion or a container memory kill. Likely causes include oversized partitions, a forced broadcast, large per-key state, Python worker memory, or insufficient overhead. Collect executor stderr, container exit reason, peak memory, spill, task input sizes, and the physical plan.

Find whether the failure repeats on the same partition. Remove an unsafe broadcast hint, reduce per-task working sets, or revise an aggregation before adding heap. Increase memory only with evidence about heap versus overhead. The trade-off is lower concurrency or greater cost; merely moving the failure to another worker does not fix the partition shape.'''),
('Driver out-of-memory', '''Symptom: the application loses its driver or the notebook disconnects while executors are otherwise healthy. Likely causes include collect/toPandas on a large result, huge query plans, or excessive file metadata. Collect the driver log, result-size warnings, file counts, and plan size before increasing driver memory.

Replace unbounded collection with a distributed write or a bounded sample. Compact files where metadata dominates, using a controlled rewrite and validation. Raising result-size limits can remove a protective boundary and cause a harder crash. A bigger driver is justified only after understanding the result or metadata it must hold.'''),
('Slow shuffle and data skew', '''Symptom: most tasks finish quickly while a few reducers run much longer, or fetch wait dominates the stage. Likely causes include hot keys, large shuffle volume, slow local disks, or failing network paths. Collect task-duration percentiles, per-task shuffle bytes, spill, fetch failures, and key-frequency statistics.

Compare the slow partition’s size with the median. Try preaggregation, a safe broadcast, AQE where eligible, or a targeted skew strategy. Salting requires correct recombination and special care for joins; increasing partitions alone does not divide a single hot key. Test result equivalence and monitor output file counts after changing the exchange.'''),
('Garbage collection and failed stages', '''Symptom: long GC pauses with low useful throughput, repeated task loss, or a stage that retries until failure. Likely causes include excessive object allocation, oversized task state, unstable executors, source read failures, and incompatible code. Collect GC logs if enabled, executor loss reasons, the first causal exception, and retry patterns by partition.

Prefer built-in expressions and bounded state when they reduce allocation. For repeated failures, distinguish deterministic bad input from a transient infrastructure issue. Validate or quarantine malformed records under an explicit completeness policy. Increasing task retry counts can amplify load and delay a deterministic failure; it is not a fix for bad data or missing classes.'''),
('Resource contention and small files', '''Symptom: long scheduling delay, low active-task counts, slow listing, or many tiny tasks. Likely causes include shared-queue pressure, dynamic-allocation limits, too many output partitions, or overly granular storage partitioning. Collect scheduler/queue metrics, executor allocation history, file counts and sizes, and scan planning time.

Separate time waiting for resources from time executing tasks. Change queue allocation or schedule only when contention is established. Compact small files with a validated rewrite and rollback plan; coalescing aggressively can create an oversized task and reduce read parallelism. The trade-off is between metadata overhead, rewrite cost, and useful future query parallelism.'''),
('A repeatable investigation record', '''For every case, write symptoms, likely causes, evidence, investigation steps, candidate solutions, and risks. Identify an observation that could disprove the leading hypothesis. Record input snapshot, application ID, Spark build, configuration, and output checks so another engineer can reproduce the comparison.

The local lab provides a healthy reference plan; none of these production failures was induced in this task. Practice a bounded failure drill in a disposable environment, then confirm both infrastructure health and the dataset’s business cutoff after remediation. A completed retry is insufficient if the published totals or freshness remain wrong.''')],
['Symptom → scope / application ID', 'Logs + plan + task distributions', 'Hypothesis → bounded change → correctness and deadline checks'],
'What evidence would distinguish heap exhaustion from a container overhead kill on your platform?')

article('spark-cloudera-cloud', 'Spark on Cloudera and Cloud Platforms: Match the Operating Model', 'Cloud Architecture',
'Compare YARN, Kubernetes, and managed Spark by identity, storage, isolation, lifecycle, and recovery responsibilities.', [
('The execution model persists; ownership changes', '''Driver planning, executor tasks, shuffles, and application-level correctness remain relevant across platforms. What changes is who allocates resources, patches the runtime, configures identity, operates storage connectors, and responds when a workload fails. Compare those responsibilities before comparing a headline hourly rate.

Use the exact supported runtime matrix for the distribution or service. An upstream Spark tutorial is not permission to replace a vendor-managed runtime or bypass its supported connectors. Python, Java, Scala, Hadoop libraries, and table formats must agree with the target platform. Run a compatibility fixture through the actual service before planning migration.'''),
('Cloudera and YARN', '''In a YARN environment, queues, application placement, driver deploy mode, and NodeManager resources affect availability and fairness. Kerberos and platform authorization may govern access to data and services. Dynamic allocation also depends on the distribution’s supported shuffle-preservation mechanism and configuration.

Collect queue waiting time separately from task runtime. A slow application may be waiting for shared capacity rather than suffering a query regression. HDFS locality can benefit some workloads, but it does not excuse poor partitioning or unbounded driver results. Work with administrators on resource and security policies rather than embedding privileged credentials in notebooks.'''),
('Kubernetes', '''Kubernetes schedules driver and executor pods, while Spark still schedules tasks inside the application. Service accounts, RBAC, image dependencies, network reachability, and local storage limits become explicit design inputs. A pending executor pod is a different problem from a running executor with a slow task.

Use reproducible images and supported dependency injection. Evaluate pod startup time, image pulls, shuffle storage lifetime, and executor loss behavior. Ephemeral storage can make recovery and shuffle recomputation expensive; persistent options add cost and operational decisions. Kubernetes does not automatically make a batch workload elastic or cheaper.'''),
('Managed cloud services and object storage', '''Managed cluster services such as Google Cloud Dataproc on Compute Engine and Amazon EMR on EC2 retain cluster-level sizing and lifecycle choices; serverless variants move more provisioning responsibility to the service. Managed services can take over cluster provisioning and some runtime operations. They still leave workload correctness, permissions, data layout, quotas, and cost control with the application owner. Serverless execution changes allocation and billing boundaries; it does not eliminate skew or a remote source bottleneck.

Object storage access depends on connector and commit protocol semantics. Do not assume HDFS rename behavior or local metadata latency. Validate partition discovery, write publication, retries, and failure cleanup using the destination’s supported committer or table format. IAM scope, network egress, and regional placement affect both risk and total cost.'''),
('A migration acceptance contract', '''Use a non-confidential representative fixture with duplicates, late data, schema variation, null keys, and a known reconciliation total. Compare results, runtime distributions, file layout, access controls, and recovery after an interrupted write. Include a rollback path that preserves the previous published dataset.

Price idle capacity, startup latency, storage requests, cross-zone traffic, support effort, and recovery work alongside compute. The local PySpark lab validates structured computation only; no Cloudera, YARN, Kubernetes, or managed-cloud deployment was executed here. Platform-specific commands require the service’s current documentation and an authorized test environment.''')],
['Same Spark query + correctness contract', 'YARN / Kubernetes / managed runtime responsibilities', 'Storage + identity + lifecycle + total cost acceptance'],
'Which operational responsibility would your team gain or lose by moving this job to a managed service?')

article('data-quality-foundation-for-ai', 'Why Data Engineering Is the Foundation of Enterprise AI', 'Engineering Perspectives',
'Treat freshness, permission, lineage, and reconciliation as parts of the AI product’s correctness contract.', [
('The model cannot repair the source contract', '''An assistant can produce a fluent answer from a stale customer status table. The failure begins before generation: nobody defined when that table was last reconciled, which source was authoritative, or whether the user could access every retrieved record. Better prompts can make the answer sound more careful without correcting the underlying evidence.

Define an AI answer as a chain of dependencies. Source changes must be captured, transformed, validated, indexed or made queryable, retrieved under the right permissions, and interpreted with traceable evidence. Each step introduces a possible mismatch. A model evaluation that ignores that chain can report strong benchmark scores while the production product gives outdated advice.'''),
('Make data readiness measurable', '''For each critical answer path, identify the owner, allowed data age, completeness threshold, schema expectations, and permission boundary. Record event time and ingestion time separately. A fast pipeline over a delayed source is not fresh business data. Include deletion and access-revocation propagation in the contract, especially for retrieval indexes and cached answers.

Use a small evaluation set tied to known source snapshots. Change a source fact, remove a permission, introduce a late record, and observe the resulting answer and citation. The acceptance test is not only “did retrieval return text?” It is whether the authorized user receives a current, supported answer or an explicit inability to answer.'''),
('Connect investment to an actual decision', '''Suppose a support assistant recommends actions using account entitlements. Measure unsupported recommendations, manual corrections, time to resolution, and the fraction of answers backed by current authorized evidence. Segment by answer type; an average can hide failures in a high-risk workflow.

Do not assign all support improvement to AI when routing, staffing, or documentation changed at the same time. Compare a stable cohort or a staged rollout, and report the residual uncertainty. Data controls have business value when they reduce correction effort or decision risk at an acceptable operating cost, not simply because the platform has more checks.'''),
('An operating model that survives change', '''Keep source owners in the release loop when schemas, definitions, or access policies change. Version transformations and retrieval configuration alongside evaluation fixtures. Alert on source-to-index freshness and reconciliation failures, and define when the assistant should decline a request rather than offer an unsupported answer.

Begin with one answer path and a recoverable failure policy. Expanding the model’s tool access before establishing data ownership increases the number of systems through which an error can travel. A useful launch gate is evidence that the system detects an invalid data condition and fails in the agreed way. The existing project notes below provide concrete context without claiming a production deployment.''')],
['Owned source → validated transformations', 'Authorized retrieval + current evidence', 'Evaluated answer → decision → correction feedback'],
'Which data contract would have to fail before your AI assistant should stop answering?')

article('pipeline-business-failure', 'Why a Successful Pipeline Can Still Be a Business Failure', 'Engineering Perspectives',
'A green scheduler measures execution. A useful dataset must also meet a decision deadline, definition, and reconciliation contract.', [
('A completed job is an intermediate outcome', '''Imagine a campaign audience built successfully at 10:30 when the campaign cutoff was 09:00. The scheduler is green, compute utilization is healthy, and every task returned zero. The business still missed the opportunity. The same pattern appears when a revenue dataset arrives on time but includes yesterday’s currency mapping or silently excludes rejected transactions.

Start the service definition with the user’s decision. What must be true about the data, by when, for whom? Task success is one dependency of that definition. Freshness, completeness, semantic correctness, and accessible publication are others. Treating them as separate dashboards without a common owner leaves the decision itself unmonitored.'''),
('Define success at the publication boundary', '''A useful publication gate compares source counts or totals, accepted rows, rejected rows, expected partitions, and the reference-data version. It should distinguish unavailable output from available but degraded output. Publishing a partial dataset can be reasonable if the user knows its limits; silently labeling it complete is a different decision.

Make backfills explicit. A rerun that overwrites a published partition may change reports after stakeholders acted on them. Record publication versions and communicate corrections. A successful overwrite is not automatically a safe business recovery, especially when downstream systems cache or export the previous result.'''),
('Measure deadline and decision impact', '''Track the percentage of critical publications meeting their agreed cutoff, time to detect invalid output, time to restore usable output, and stakeholder reconciliation effort. Connect incidents to delayed decisions or affected customers where evidence allows. Avoid converting every late minute into invented revenue loss.

For an illustrative daily audience, report the number of campaigns delayed and the actual manual work required to recover. Compare this with the cost of tighter SLAs, extra capacity, and operational coverage. Some datasets deserve a relaxed deadline; others justify stronger guarantees. The point is an explicit service choice, not the strictest target everywhere.'''),
('Change the review conversation', '''During design review, ask who consumes the dataset, what happens when it is late, how they learn it is incomplete, and how they verify a correction. During an incident, ask whether the user can make the intended decision again, rather than closing the ticket when the retry finishes.

Introduce one publication gate on the most consequential dataset before adding hundreds of generic tests. Watch false positives and operator load. A gate that blocks good data every day can be as damaging as a missing control. Tune it against known incidents and controlled fixtures, with a named owner for overrides and follow-up.''')],
['Task success', 'Freshness + completeness + definition checks', 'Published dataset → decision before cutoff'],
'Which green pipeline in your organization could miss its business deadline without an alert?')

article('the-cost-of-untrusted-data', 'The Real Cost of Poor Data Quality', 'Engineering Perspectives',
'Build a defensible cost model from reconciliation work, decision delay, incident response, and customer correction.', [
('Begin with observable work', '''The easiest cost to observe is often the analyst who reconciles two totals every Monday. Record the hours, the systems compared, the cause, and the decisions waiting on the result. Then look for recurring support tickets, manual corrections, rerun compute, and incident response. These are evidence of friction, not yet proof of every claimed downstream loss.

Separate direct costs from risk exposure. Ten hours of measured reconciliation work is different from a hypothetical regulatory penalty. Keep assumptions visible and avoid adding overlapping categories. The same engineer’s incident hours should not appear once under rework and again under platform operations.'''),
('A transparent illustrative model', '''Suppose four analysts each spend two hours a week reconciling one critical dataset. That is eight hours weekly. At an explicitly assumed loaded cost of 50 currency units per hour, the direct labor estimate is 400 units per week. Add measured rerun compute and support work separately. This example is arithmetic, not a claim about any employer or project.

Now identify what a control could actually remove. If only half the reconciliation work comes from the targeted defect, the addressable labor is about 200 units weekly before implementation and operating costs. A realistic investment case compares that bounded benefit with maintenance effort, false alerts, and the risk of blocking publication.'''),
('Prioritize by consequence and recurrence', '''A cosmetic formatting problem and an incorrect account entitlement deserve different urgency. Rank datasets by the decisions they support, defect frequency, detection delay, and recovery difficulty. A small table used in many financial rules may deserve more attention than a huge exploratory dataset.

Choose controls linked to observed failure modes: uniqueness for duplicate business keys, reconciliation for missing records, freshness checks for delayed feeds, and referential checks for broken joins. Schema validation alone cannot establish that a total is correct. Conversely, a broad rule with no owner can create alert fatigue without reducing harm.'''),
('Prove improvement without double counting', '''Establish a baseline and compare a stable period after the change. Track reconciliation hours, recurring incidents, detection time, affected publications, and control maintenance effort. If a source migration or team change occurs simultaneously, state that the before/after comparison cannot isolate the control’s contribution.

Keep the business owner in the review. A technically impressive validation service has limited value if the decision process still requires the same manual checks. The goal is a smaller, demonstrable burden and a clearer recovery path. The earlier operating-model notes remain below because ownership and recoverability are what make the cost reduction durable.''')],
['Observed defect → rework / delay / correction', 'Bounded addressable cost − control operating cost', 'Baseline → change → measured net outcome'],
'Which quality defect has the largest measured recurring cost, rather than the loudest complaint?')

article('architecture-cost-risk', 'Why Architecture Decisions Must Include Cost and Risk', 'Engineering Perspectives',
'Compare designs using total operating cost, failure exposure, reversibility, and evidence that would change the decision.', [
('A design is a set of obligations', '''A proposal for cross-region replication can sound obviously safer until the team prices traffic, conflict handling, operational complexity, and recovery testing. A cheaper single-region design can sound efficient until the business states an outage tolerance it cannot meet. Neither the lowest invoice nor the most redundant diagram settles the decision.

Write the constraints first: recovery time, acceptable data loss, latency, residency, staffing, and budget. Distinguish requirements from preferences and identify who owns each one. An architecture decision becomes reviewable when another engineer can explain which constraint would force a different choice.'''),
('Compare total cost under more than one scenario', '''Include steady-state compute, storage, requests, network transfer, licenses, support, operational effort, and migration. Then add recovery scenarios: a failed zone, a backlog rebuild, a schema rollback, and a peak business period. A design with cheap steady state may have expensive or slow recovery.

Use ranges for uncertain inputs and show which assumptions dominate. A rough sensitivity analysis is more honest than a precise total built from guesses. If doubling traffic or losing one specialist changes the preferred design, make that dependency visible. The review should identify the next measurement that reduces uncertainty.'''),
('Risk is probability, consequence, and control', '''Do not multiply invented failure probabilities into a confident expected-loss number. Use observed incidents where available and explicit qualitative scenarios elsewhere. State the blast radius, detection mechanism, recovery procedure, and test evidence for each material failure mode.

A replication feature is a control only when failover and reconciliation have been exercised under the relevant conditions. A backup that has never been restored is an unvalidated recovery assumption. Track residual risk after controls, and assign someone who can accept or reject it. Technical teams should not silently make a business risk decision through a configuration default.'''),
('Make the decision reversible where possible', '''Document the selected option, rejected alternatives, assumptions, review date, and trigger conditions. Examples include a sustained traffic threshold, an unacceptable recovery drill result, or a staffing change. Prefer an incremental migration with a measurable rollback boundary when it reduces exposure without unacceptable delay.

For a data-platform move, run representative queries and interrupted-write tests before switching publication. Price parallel operation and reconciliation, not just the target cluster. The cheapest migration plan on paper can be the one that leaves no safe path back when a connector or permission model behaves differently.''')],
['Constraints + alternatives', 'Steady state + recovery cost + residual risk', 'Decision record → trigger → revisit / rollback'],
'Which assumption in your latest architecture decision would most likely reverse the recommendation?')

article('ai-dataops-investigation-copilot', 'AI Agents for Distributed Systems Troubleshooting', 'Engineering Perspectives',
'Keep diagnosis evidence-linked, tools bounded, and remediation subject to explicit policy and recovery checks.', [
('The useful unit is a supported hypothesis', '''An incident assistant that confidently says “restart the broker” has not yet helped. The useful output is a scoped hypothesis with evidence: affected partitions, a matching time window, relevant errors, competing explanations, and the next observation that would distinguish them. The operator must be able to inspect the underlying telemetry.

Distributed systems produce correlated symptoms. Lag may rise because a sink slowed, a consumer rebalanced, a broker lost disk throughput, or the producer surged. A language model can organize evidence and retrieve a runbook, but correlation does not establish the causal path. Preserve uncertainty rather than turning a ranked guess into an automatic command.'''),
('Separate read access from change authority', '''Start with read-only tools scoped to the incident. Bound queries by time, resource, and output size. Treat log text and retrieved documents as untrusted data; they must not become tool instructions. Keep credentials outside prompts and redact sensitive payloads before sending evidence to any model service.

A proposed change should identify target, command or API operation, preconditions, expected effect, rollback, and approval policy. Enforce those limits in the tool layer rather than relying on the model to remember them. A familiar runbook is not permission to restart a production service without the environment’s actual authorization rules.'''),
('Evaluate the assistant as an incident system', '''Build replay fixtures with known causes and cases where the evidence is insufficient. Compare supported-diagnosis time, false hypotheses, unsafe-action proposals, unresolved incidents, and recovery verification. Run a rule-only or human-only baseline on the same fixtures. Faster text generation is not the same as faster safe recovery.

Measure operational cost as well: model latency, retrieval maintenance, telemetry access, and reviewer effort. An assistant that floods the operator with plausible explanations may increase incident load. Score whether it asks for a discriminating observation and whether its final recommendation remains consistent with the collected evidence.'''),
('Keep business recovery in the loop', '''After an approved action, check the original symptom, redundancy, backlog clearance, and dataset freshness. A lower CPU graph does not prove that delayed customer events are usable again. Attach the recovery evidence to the incident record and record unresolved risk.

The InvestiNator notes below describe a local demo with synthetic telemetry and simulated recovery. They are useful project context, not proof of a production autonomous operator. No production remediation was executed here. That distinction protects both the reader and the credibility of the engineering argument.''')],
['Read-only telemetry → evidence-linked hypotheses', 'Policy + operator approval → bounded action', 'Recovery telemetry → dataset freshness → incident record'],
'What observation would make your incident assistant withdraw its leading diagnosis?')

article('reliability-business-capability', 'Engineering Reliability as a Business Capability', 'Engineering Perspectives',
'Use service objectives, recovery drills, ownership, and error-budget decisions to make dependable operations an explicit investment.', [
('Reliability is the ability to keep a promise', '''A dashboard that loads quickly but shows yesterday’s inventory is available in one sense and unusable in another. Define the promise at the user boundary: a fresh dataset, a completed payment, an accurate recommendation, or a recoverable publication. Infrastructure uptime is an input to that promise, not its full definition.

Ask the business owner which failures are tolerable, for how long, and with what fallback. An internal exploration tool and a time-critical operational feed need different service levels. Applying the strictest objective everywhere spends money and attention without a corresponding benefit.'''),
('Turn objectives into operating choices', '''Choose a service-level indicator that reflects the promise, such as the fraction of critical publications delivered correctly before their cutoff. Define the measurement window and exclusions before an incident. Include how correctness is checked and what happens when the check itself is unavailable.

An error budget can guide release and reliability work when its policy is agreed. If the budget is exhausted, the response might be reducing risky changes, fixing recurring defects, or improving recovery. It should not become a ritual percentage disconnected from user harm. Review whether alerts identify actionable threats early enough to matter.'''),
('Recovery is a practiced capability', '''A written runbook has value only if responders can use it with their actual permissions and tools. Exercise restoration, replay, rollback, and reconciliation in a safe environment. Record elapsed time, missing access, ambiguous steps, and remaining data gaps. A failed drill is useful evidence before the real outage.

Design ownership across team boundaries. A data product may depend on source applications, platform storage, transformation code, and downstream publication. Name the incident coordinator and escalation path. Without that agreement, each component can appear healthy while the end-to-end service stays broken.'''),
('Make the investment case with outcomes', '''Measure recurrence, detection time, time to restore usable service, manual work, and missed business deadlines. Compare those outcomes with the cost of on-call coverage, spare capacity, tooling, and engineering time. Avoid promising zero incidents; show which failure modes the investment reduces and how recovery becomes more predictable.

Review reliability work after implementation. If an alert never changes an action, revise or remove it. If spare capacity does not clear the expected backlog within the recovery objective, the model needs updating. Reliability becomes a business capability when the organization can choose a service promise and repeatedly demonstrate that it can keep or restore it.''')],
['User promise → measurable service objective', 'Ownership + controls + recovery practice', 'Observed outcomes → investment / release decisions'],
'Which recovery promise has your team demonstrated rather than only documented?')
